    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Solidarity</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prague-spring.jpg" alt="Prague Spring" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Prague Spring <span class="kid-tale-cr">The Central Intelligence Agency, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>In the 1970s Poland was ruled by one party and was under the power of the Soviet Union.</p><p>Armies led by the Soviet Union had crushed the Hungarian Uprising and the Prague Spring.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gdansk.jpg" alt="Gdańsk" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Gdańsk <span class="kid-tale-cr">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>In August 1980, workers at the shipyard in Gdańsk went on strike after Anna Walentynowicz lost her job.</p><p>The government agreed to let them form Solidarity, a free trade union, a group that speaks up for workers.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lech-walesa.jpg" alt="Lech Wałęsa" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Lech Wałęsa <span class="kid-tale-cr">MEDEF, CC BY-SA 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Lech Wałęsa led the union, and by 1981 it had 10 million members.</p><p>In December 1981 the government brought in martial law, strict rule by force, and jailed the union’s leaders.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize.jpg" alt="Nobel Peace Prize" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Nobel Peace Prize <span class="kid-tale-cr">Adam Jones from Kelowna, BC, Canada, CC BY-SA 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Solidarity carried on in secret, and in 1983 Wałęsa won the Nobel Peace Prize.</p><p>Solidarity always tried to reach its aims without violence.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>In 1989 Solidarity won almost every seat it could stand for, and this helped set off the Revolutions of 1989.</p><p>Today Solidarity is a trade union again; in 2010 it had more than 400,000 members.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born of the August 1980 strike at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> Shipyard, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> grew to 10 million members by September 1981, a third of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>’s working-age population.</li><li>Martial law, imposed in December 1981 under General Wojciech Jaruzelski, drove the union underground, where it survived with substantial financial support from the Vatican and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</li><li>The Round Table Talks of 1989 led to semi-free elections that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> swept, setting off a chain reaction across the Soviet bloc known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">Revolutions of 1989</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>At its height <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> had over 10 million members, the largest union membership in the world, and the philosopher Leszek Kołakowski called it a working-class revolution “directed against a socialist state, and carried out under the sign of the cross, with the blessing of the Pope.”</p></aside>
    </section>

